::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Digital presentation purpose: Explain how peer feedback improves writing. Which first slide is strongest? \{
~A. A thank-you slide#Incorrect. A thank-you slide belongs near the end, not first.
~B. A random image without a label#Incorrect. An unlabeled image does not establish coherent ideas.
~C. A list of unrelated school topics#Incorrect. This would weaken focus immediately.
=D. A title slide with the topic and clear perspective#Correct. The first slide should orient the audience to the presentation's main idea.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.05 U06 L04 Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Presentation purpose: Explain steps for preparing for a long-term assignment. Which structure fits best? \{
~A. Compare-contrast#Incorrect. The task asks for steps, not similarities and differences.
~B. Random order#Incorrect. Random order makes a process hard to follow.
=C. Sequence#Correct. A step-by-step process should use sequence structure.
~D. Problem only#Incorrect. The task asks for preparation steps, not just a problem.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.05
Which slide order is most coherent for a problem-solution presentation? \{
=A. Perspective, problem, solution, steps, final takeaway#Correct. This sequence develops a clear problem-solution structure.
~B. Solution, thank you, unrelated image, problem#Incorrect. This order is confusing and includes unrelated material.
~C. Title, final takeaway, problem, random fact#Incorrect. The final takeaway appears too early and the random fact weakens coherence.
~D. Image, image, image, closing#Incorrect. Images alone do not develop coherent ideas.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.03 U06 L04 Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.03
Which slide note best explains information clearly? \{
~A. Stuff about assignments#Incorrect. This is vague and not explanatory.
~B. Due dates are a thing#Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
~C. Whatever comes next#Incorrect. This shows no organization.
=D. Break the assignment into smaller tasks for each day of the week.#Correct. This explains a clear idea in a concise slide note.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
A slide sequence includes title, problem, solution, example, final takeaway. What makes it coherent? \{
~A. The slides can be shuffled without changing the message.#Incorrect. If order does not matter, the structure is probably weak.
=B. Each slide has a clear job that develops the same perspective.#Correct. Coherence comes from connected slide purposes.
~C. The slides avoid all organization.#Incorrect. Coherence requires organization.
~D. The slides include unrelated decorations.#Incorrect. Decorations do not create coherence.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.05 U06 L04 Q06::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Presentation purpose: Show how one habit leads to better preparation. Which structure fits best? \{
~A. Alphabetical order#Incorrect. Alphabetical order would not show cause and effect.
~B. Random order#Incorrect. Random order would weaken meaning.
=C. Cause-effect#Correct. The purpose asks how one thing leads to another result.
~D. Unrelated list#Incorrect. A list without structure would not show the relationship.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q07::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.05
Which slide should be removed from a presentation about using feedback before final submission? \{
~A. Slide: Feedback can identify unclear claims.#Incorrect. This supports the presentation topic.
=B. Slide: The cafeteria has several lunch options.#Correct. This is unrelated and would break coherence.
~C. Slide: Revision after feedback can improve clarity.#Incorrect. This supports the presentation topic.
~D. Slide: Final work should reflect useful feedback.#Incorrect. This supports the presentation topic.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L04 Q08::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.03
How should the speaker use the digital slides? \{
~A. Read every word on every slide without explaining.#Incorrect. Slides should support oral communication, not replace it.
~B. Ignore the slide order during the presentation.#Incorrect. The slide order helps the audience follow the message.
~C. Add unrelated pictures to fill time.#Incorrect. Unrelated images distract from coherent ideas.
=D. Use slides as organized support while explaining ideas orally.#Correct. Digital presentation structure should support the speaker's explanation.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q09::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Which digital presentation plan is weakest? \{
=A. Title, random picture, unrelated fact, different topic, closing#Correct. This plan lacks a clear structure and coherent ideas.
~B. Topic, perspective, two connected reasons, final takeaway#Incorrect. This is a coherent plan.
~C. Problem, solution, example, final takeaway#Incorrect. This is a logical structure.
~D. Step 1, Step 2, Step 3, final reminder#Incorrect. This is coherent for a process.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.03 U06 L04 Q10::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Which transition would help connect slides in an expository presentation? \{
~A. "That was random, so here is something else."#Incorrect. This admits lack of connection.
~B. "Forget the last slide."#Incorrect. This weakens coherence.
~C. "The next slide has no connection."#Incorrect. This breaks the presentation's structure.
=D. "Now I will explain the next step in the process."#Correct. This transition signals organization and helps the audience follow.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q11::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.05
A presentation's final slide should usually do what? \{
~A. Introduce an unrelated topic#Incorrect. A new unrelated topic weakens closure.
~B. Hide the main point#Incorrect. The main point should be clear.
=C. Return to the clear perspective or final takeaway#Correct. The final slide should help the audience remember the main idea.
~D. Replace the whole presentation with decoration#Incorrect. Decoration cannot replace meaning.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.05 U06 L04 Q12::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.03
Which structure is best for showing similarities and differences between paper planners and digital calendars? \{
~A. Problem-solution#Incorrect. The task asks for similarities and differences, not a problem and solution.
=B. Compare-contrast#Correct. Compare-contrast organizes similarities and differences.
~C. Cause-effect#Incorrect. Cause-effect explains causes and results.
~D. Random order#Incorrect. Random order would not clarify comparison.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q13::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Which slide note is too wordy for a digital presentation slide? \{
=A. Students should remember that in many cases there are several different things they might possibly need to think about when they are deciding how to plan assignments over a long period of time.#Correct. This is too long and would overload a slide.
~B. Step 1: List upcoming due dates.#Incorrect. This is concise, so it would work as a slide note and is not the too-wordy option.
~C. Final takeaway: Planning makes deadlines manageable.#Incorrect. This is concise and useful.
~D. Problem: Missed deadlines create stress.#Incorrect. This is concise and clear, so it would not overload a digital presentation slide.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L04 Q14::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Why should slide order match the speaker's oral explanation? \{
~A. So the slides can distract from the speaker#Incorrect. Slides should support, not distract.
~B. So the speaker can skip the main idea#Incorrect. The main idea should remain clear.
=C. So the audience receives the same organized message visually and orally#Correct. Alignment between slides and speech helps the audience follow.
~D. So the presentation becomes less coherent#Incorrect. Matching order increases coherence.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.03 U06 L04 Q15::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.05
Which slide title best fits an explanatory presentation about assignment planning? \{
~A. Random Things#Incorrect. This is vague and not explanatory.
~B. Maybe This Matters#Incorrect. This lacks clarity and confidence.
~C. Pictures and Stuff#Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
=D. Step 2: Break the Assignment into Smaller Tasks#Correct. This title clearly signals an explanatory step.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q16::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.03
A student asks, "What job does this slide do?" What is the student checking? \{
=A. Whether the slide supports coherent presentation structure#Correct. Each slide should have a job connected to the presentation's ideas.
~B. Whether the slide has enough decoration#Incorrect. The question is about purpose, not decoration.
~C. Whether the slide can be unrelated#Incorrect. The student is checking against unrelated content.
~D. Whether the speaker can ignore organization#Incorrect. The question supports organization.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.05 U06 L04 Q17::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Presentation purpose: Explain why missed deadlines happen and what results from them. Which structure fits best? \{
~A. Compare-contrast#Incorrect. The task is not comparing two things.
=B. Cause-effect#Correct. The purpose asks for reasons and results.
~C. Alphabetical order#Incorrect. Alphabetical order would not show cause and effect.
~D. Random order#Incorrect. Random order would weaken meaning.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q18::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Which slide best supports a clear perspective about feedback improving writing? \{
~A. Slide: Favorite classroom colors#Incorrect. This is unrelated to feedback improving writing and would not support the presentation's perspective.
~B. Slide: Lunch schedule changes#Incorrect. This shifts to a different school topic and does not support feedback improving writing.
=C. Slide: Feedback helps writers identify unclear evidence before final submission#Correct. This slide directly supports the perspective.
~D. Slide: Sports scores from last week#Incorrect. This is unrelated to writing feedback and would break the digital presentation's coherence.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.03 U06 L04 Q19::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.05
Which outline best explains a process? \{
~A. Final thought, unrelated story, title, image#Incorrect. This is not a clear process.
~B. Random fact, random fact, random fact#Incorrect. This lacks organization.
~C. Thank you, title, problem, unrelated image#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
=D. Step 1, Step 2, Step 3, final reminder#Correct. This outline uses sequence to explain a process.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q20::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.03
A presentation has coherent ideas when the slides do what? \{
~A. Use as many unrelated images as possible#Incorrect. Unrelated images weaken coherence.
~B. Avoid any structure#Incorrect. Structure is needed for coherence.
=C. Build one clear perspective in a logical order#Correct. Coherent slides connect to the same purpose and sequence.
~D. Change topics every slide#Incorrect. Changing topics breaks coherence.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L04 Q21::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Which speaker note best connects a slide to oral explanation? \{
~A. "I do not know why this slide is here."#Incorrect. This shows the slide lacks purpose.
~B. "Ignore this slide."#Incorrect. A slide that should be ignored probably should be removed.
~C. "This picture is unrelated but colorful."#Incorrect. Color does not replace purpose.
=D. "This slide shows the problem I will solve in the next part of the presentation."#Correct. This note connects the slide to the presentation structure.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.05 U06 L04 Q22::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Which structure would best organize a presentation about two different study tools? \{
=A. Compare-contrast#Correct. Comparing two tools calls for similarities and differences.
~B. Sequence#Incorrect. Sequence is best for steps in order.
~C. Cause-effect#Incorrect. Cause-effect is best for causes and results.
~D. Problem only#Incorrect. The task asks about two tools, not only a problem.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q23::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.05
Which revision improves a weak slide sequence? Weak sequence: title, random image, unrelated quote, final thanks. \{
~A. Add more random images.#Incorrect. More unrelated visuals would not improve coherence.
=B. Replace the middle slides with problem, solution, and example slides.#Correct. This adds a logical structure that develops the idea.
~C. Remove the title and keep the unrelated quote.#Incorrect. The presentation still lacks coherent development.
~D. Put the thank-you slide first.#Incorrect. That would make the order less logical.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.03 U06 L04 Q24::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.03
Which sentence best explains why a sequence structure fits a how-to presentation? \{
~A. A sequence structure hides the steps from the audience.#Incorrect. The purpose is to make steps clear.
=B. A sequence structure lists steps in the order the audience should follow them.#Correct. A how-to presentation needs ordered steps.
~C. A sequence structure compares two unrelated topics.#Incorrect. That describes compare-contrast more than sequence.
~D. A sequence structure removes the need for a clear perspective.#Incorrect. A clear perspective is still needed.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L04 Q25::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Which question should a student ask when checking digital presentation coherence? \{
=A. Does each slide support the same perspective and connect to the slide before or after it?#Correct. This checks both focus and logical sequence.
~B. Does every slide use the same font size, even if ideas are unrelated?#Incorrect. Formatting consistency helps appearance, but it does not guarantee coherence.
~C. Can I add a funny picture to every slide?#Incorrect. Humor does not guarantee coherent ideas.
~D. Did I include every fact I found?#Incorrect. Including every fact can overload and weaken coherence.
}
